title: what the vault holds
pid: 47f57fda-1b33-4b55-b69b-16ceca89e96b
body: |2-
  The vault holds a folder named after the skgrepo and a =README.md=
  that explains the format to readers. The README's properties say the
  version of the format (=skg-format=) and, for each folder, the
  skgrepo it holds, that skgrepo's remote hash and its commit
  (=skg-folders=, =skg-skgrepos=, =skg-remote-hashes=, =skg-commits=,
  lists with one entry per folder). There is no hidden manifest.
  Each file of the folder is a /document/: a note that no other note of
  the skgrepo contains, or one that forks something, with the tree it
  contains. A cycle that no such note reaches makes a document of its
  smallest ID. The file is named after the root's title, made safe for
  every filesystem, followed by the first eight characters of its ID,
  as in =Feelings (41ec1d8d).md=.
  The root's title is the file's heading, and its ID, aliases and other
  metadata are properties. Every other note is a bullet ending in its ID
  as a block ID (=^ID=), with its body indented beneath it and the notes
  it contains nested beneath that. A note reached a second time (one
  with several containers, or the step that closes a cycle) is written
  where it is first reached, and is an embed (=![[File#^ID]]=)
  everywhere else. A bullet's metadata rides in a comment,
  =%%skg {...}%%=, which Obsidian's reading view hides.
  Links become =[[File#^ID|label]]=. A link to a note outside the vault
  points to the note "Some links might be broken." when the vault has
  it, else to the README, and keeps the ID in a comment after it.
  Titles and bodies are Org, and their markup is written as the
  Markdown that shows it: bold and italic, verbatim and code as code
  spans, links to web pages as Markdown links, =#+begin_src= and
  =#+begin_example= blocks and fixed-width lines as code blocks fenced
  by tildes (whose first line says what the block was), and the rule
  under a table's first row as a Markdown table's. A body's own Markdown
  code spans, fences of backticks and tables are kept. Other Org markup
  (links to files, tables without a rule, other blocks) stays as text.
  Characters that Markdown would read as structure (=[[=, =%%=, =^=,
  =#=, =$= and a few more), or as markup made from Org (backticks,
  asterisks), are escaped with a backslash.
  Each title and body is read back before it is written. One whose
  conversions do not read back exactly is written without them; one
  the escapes cannot give back exactly is written verbatim in a fenced
  block, or a property. The summary counts the markup converted, what
  was left as text and why, and the verbatim fallbacks.
